    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Reaction Equations and Redox|Writing and balancing equations",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "A balanced chemical equation contains equal numbers of every type of atom on the reactant and product sides.",
        "Write correct formulae first, count atoms, adjust whole-number coefficients and reduce them to the smallest ratio. Coefficients multiply complete formula units; subscripts belong to substance identities and must not be altered during balancing.",
        "For Al + O2 -> Al2O3, balance aluminium and oxygen to obtain 4Al + 3O2 -> 2Al2O3, then verify four Al and six O atoms on each side.",
        "unbalanced: Fe + H2O -> Fe3O4 + H2; balanced: 3Fe + 4H2O -> Fe3O4 + 4H2",
        "Never change a subscript such as H2O into H2O2 to balance atoms; that changes water into a different substance.",
        "Balanced equations support quantitative reaction calculations, manufacturing yields and pollution accounting.",
        listOf("For Al + O2 -> Al2O3, balance aluminium and oxygen to obtain 4Al + 3O2 -> 2Al2O3, then verify four Al and six O atoms on each side.","unbalanced: Fe + H2O -> Fe3O4 + H2; balanced: 3Fe + 4H2O -> Fe3O4 + 4H2","Write correct formulae first, count atoms, adjust whole-number coefficients and reduce them to the smallest ratio.","Coefficients multiply complete formula units","subscripts belong to substance identities and must not be altered during balancing.","Never change a subscript such as H2O into H2O2 to balance atoms; that changes water into a different substance."),
        listOf("Conservation of mass","Coefficients","Stoichiometry","Balancing chemical equations")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Reaction Equations and Redox|Combination and decomposition reactions",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "A combination reaction forms one main product from two or more reactants.\n\nA decomposition reaction breaks one compound into two or more simpler substances.",
        "Atoms are rearranged into a more complex product while total atoms and mass remain conserved.\n\nEnergy from heat, light or electricity is often needed to break bonds in the reactant.",
        "2Mg + O2 -> 2MgO combines magnesium and oxygen to form magnesium oxide.\n\nExample: CaCO3 -> CaO + CO2 when limestone is strongly heated.",
        "A + B -> AB\n\nAB -> A + B",
        "A reaction with several products is not a simple combination reaction.\n\nMelting is not decomposition because it forms no new substance.",
        "Combination reactions occur in material manufacture, corrosion and synthesis.\n\nDecomposition is used to make lime, extract substances and analyze compounds.",
        listOf("2Mg + O2 -> 2MgO combines magnesium and oxygen to form magnesium oxide.","Example: CaCO3 -> CaO + CO2 when limestone is strongly heated.","A + B -> AB\n\nAB -> A + B","Atoms are rearranged into a more complex product while total atoms and mass remain conserved.","Energy from heat, light or electricity is often needed to break bonds in the reactant.","A reaction with several products is not a simple combination reaction.\n\nMelting is not decomposition because it forms no new substance."),
        listOf("Synthesis reactions","Balancing equations","Conservation of mass","Thermal decomposition","Electrolysis","Photodecomposition","Combination","Decomposition")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Reaction Equations and Redox|Displacement and precipitation reactions",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "In a displacement reaction, a more reactive element replaces a less reactive element in a compound.\n\nA precipitation reaction forms an insoluble solid when ions from aqueous solutions combine.",
        "The reactivity series predicts whether the replacement will occur and electron transfer is often involved.\n\nDouble-displacement reactions exchange ions, but a reaction is driven only when a precipitate, gas or weakly ionised product forms. The net ionic equation removes spectator ions and shows the species responsible for the visible solid.",
        "Zn + CuSO4 -> ZnSO4 + Cu because zinc is more reactive than copper.\n\nExample: Pb(NO3)2(aq) + 2KI(aq) -> PbI2(s)↓ + 2KNO3(aq) gives a yellow lead-iodide precipitate.",
        "A + BC -> AC + B\n\nAg+(aq) + Cl-(aq) -> AgCl(s)↓",
        "A less reactive metal cannot normally displace a more reactive metal from its salt solution.\n\nDo not call every double-displacement mixture a precipitation reaction; first check whether an insoluble product actually forms.",
        "Displacement reactions are used in metal extraction, batteries and reactivity tests.\n\nPrecipitation is used in ion identification, water treatment, pigment preparation and removal of contaminants.",
        listOf("Zn + CuSO4 -> ZnSO4 + Cu because zinc is more reactive than copper.","Example: Pb(NO3)2(aq) + 2KI(aq) -> PbI2(s)↓ + 2KNO3(aq) gives a yellow lead-iodide precipitate.","A + BC -> AC + B\n\nAg+(aq) + Cl-(aq) -> AgCl(s)↓","The reactivity series predicts whether the replacement will occur and electron transfer is often involved.","Double-displacement reactions exchange ions, but a reaction is driven only when a precipitate, gas or weakly ionised product forms.","The net ionic equation removes spectator ions and shows the species responsible for the visible solid."),
        listOf("Reactivity series","Redox","Single replacement","Double displacement","Solubility","Net ionic equations","Displacement","Precipitation reactions")),
