    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Equilibrium and Solutions|Dynamic chemical equilibrium",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Dynamic equilibrium occurs when forward and reverse reaction rates are equal in a closed system.",
        "Macroscopic concentrations remain constant even though both reactions continue microscopically. Equilibrium can be reached from either direction.",
        "in N2O4(g) <-> 2NO2(g), molecules continue interconverting while the colour and concentrations remain constant at equilibrium.",
        "rateforward = ratereverse",
        "Equilibrium does not mean reactions stop or that reactant and product concentrations are equal.",
        "Dynamic equilibrium controls industrial synthesis, gas binding, solubility and acid-base systems.",
        listOf("in N2O4(g) <-> 2NO2(g), molecules continue interconverting while the colour and concentrations remain constant at equilibrium.","rateforward = ratereverse","Macroscopic concentrations remain constant even though both reactions continue microscopically.","Equilibrium can be reached from either direction.","Equilibrium does not mean reactions stop or that reactant and product concentrations are equal."),
        listOf("Reversible reactions","Equilibrium constant","Reaction rates","Dynamic equilibrium")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Equilibrium and Solutions|Le Chatelier’s principle",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Le Chatelier's principle predicts how an equilibrium responds to a disturbance.",
        "The system shifts in the direction that partly opposes a concentration, pressure or temperature change. A catalyst speeds approach but does not shift equilibrium.",
        "increasing pressure in N2 + 3H2 <-> 2NH3 shifts equilibrium toward ammonia because that side has fewer gas moles.",
        "disturbance -> shift that reduces its effect",
        "Do not say a catalyst changes K or equilibrium yield; it accelerates both directions.",
        "The principle guides ammonia manufacture, gas reactions, buffers and product recovery.",
        listOf("increasing pressure in N2 + 3H2 <-> 2NH3 shifts equilibrium toward ammonia because that side has fewer gas moles.","disturbance -> shift that reduces its effect","The system shifts in the direction that partly opposes a concentration, pressure or temperature change.","A catalyst speeds approach but does not shift equilibrium.","Do not say a catalyst changes K or equilibrium yield; it accelerates both directions."),
        listOf("Reaction quotient","Equilibrium constant","Industrial optimization","Le Chatelier principle")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Equilibrium and Solutions|Solubility and concentration",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Solubility is the maximum amount of solute that dissolves in a stated amount of solvent at specified conditions.\n\nConcentration expresses how much solute is present relative to a defined amount of solution or solvent.",
        "It depends on solute-solvent interactions, temperature and, for gases, pressure. A saturated solution is in dynamic equilibrium with undissolved solute.\n\nDifferent units suit different purposes, including mass percent, molarity, molality, mole fraction and ppm.",
        "if at most 36 g NaCl dissolves in 100 g water at a temperature, adding 40 g leaves about 4 g undissolved.\n\nExample: dissolving 5 g salt to make 100 g solution gives a mass percentage of 5%.",
        "solubility = maximum dissolved solute at stated temperature and pressure\n\nmass percent = mass solute / mass solution x 100",
        "Do not confuse dissolving rate with solubility; stirring speeds dissolving but may not change the final maximum.\n\nAlways identify whether the denominator is solution, solvent, volume or mass.",
        "Solubility guides crystallization, medicines, water treatment and formulation.\n\nConcentration controls medicine doses, reaction rates, product quality and pollution limits.",
        listOf("if at most 36 g NaCl dissolves in 100 g water at a temperature, adding 40 g leaves about 4 g undissolved.","Example: dissolving 5 g salt to make 100 g solution gives a mass percentage of 5%.","solubility = maximum dissolved solute at stated temperature and pressure\n\nmass percent = mass solute / mass solution x 100","It depends on solute-solvent interactions, temperature and, for gases, pressure.","A saturated solution is in dynamic equilibrium with undissolved solute.","Different units suit different purposes, including mass percent, molarity, molality, mole fraction and ppm."),
        listOf("Saturation","Temperature effects","Intermolecular forces","Molarity","Molality","Parts per million","Solubility","Concentration")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Equilibrium and Solutions|Acids, bases and pH",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "pH is a logarithmic measure related to hydronium-ion concentration.",
        "At school level, pH below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral and above 7 is basic for water near room temperature. Each pH unit represents a tenfold concentration change.",
        "if [H3O+] = 1.0 x 10^-3 mol/L, pH = -log(1.0 x 10^-3) = 3.00. Identify the given quantities or particles, apply the relationship, and check units and chemical meaning.",
        "pH = -log10[H3O+]",
        "pH 3 is ten times more acidic by hydronium concentration than pH 4, not merely one unit more.",
        "pH control is vital in blood, soil, food, medicines, pools and wastewater.",
        listOf("if [H3O+] = 1.0 x 10^-3 mol/L, pH = -log(1.0 x 10^-3) = 3.00.","Identify the given quantities or particles, apply the relationship, and check units and chemical meaning.","pH = -log10[H3O+]","At school level, pH below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral and above 7 is basic for water near room temperature.","Each pH unit represents a tenfold concentration change.","pH 3 is ten times more acidic by hydronium concentration than pH 4, not merely one unit more."),
        listOf("Hydronium concentration","Logarithms","Acid strength","pH")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Equilibrium and Solutions|Buffers and titration curves",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "A buffer resists large pH changes when small amounts of acid or base are added.\n\nVolumetric analysis determines analyte amount from the accurately measured volume of a standardized reagent.",
        "It commonly contains a weak acid and its conjugate base, which consume added OH- and H3O+ respectively. Buffer capacity is limited.\n\nDetermines analyte amount from the accurately measured volume of a standardized reagent.",
        "an ethanoic acid/ethanoate buffer uses CH3COOH to neutralize added OH- and CH3COO- to consume added H3O+.\n\nAt equivalence, 25.00 mL of 0.1000 M HCl requires 25.00 mL of 0.1000 M NaOH.",
        "pH approximately pKa + log([base]/[acid])\n\nmoles titrant / coefficient = moles analyte / coefficient",
        "A buffer does not keep pH perfectly fixed and fails when too much acid or base is added.\n\nThe indicator endpoint is an observed signal and may not coincide exactly with the stoichiometric equivalence point.",
        "Buffers stabilize blood, medicines, foods, fermentation and analytical solutions.\n\nA standardized titrant determines an unknown solution amount from measured endpoint volume and the reaction stoichiometry.",
        listOf("an ethanoic acid/ethanoate buffer uses CH3COOH to neutralize added OH- and CH3COO- to consume added H3O+.","At equivalence, 25.00 mL of 0.1000 M HCl requires 25.00 mL of 0.1000 M NaOH.","pH approximately pKa + log([base]/[acid])\n\nmoles titrant / coefficient = moles analyte / coefficient","It commonly contains a weak acid and its conjugate base, which consume added OH- and H3O+ respectively.","Buffer capacity is limited.","Determines analyte amount from the accurately measured volume of a standardized reagent."),
        listOf("Conjugate pairs","Weak acids","Buffer capacity","Buffers","Volumetric analysis")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Kinetics and Electrochemistry|Reaction rate and rate laws",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "A rate law relates reaction rate to reactant concentrations through experimentally determined powers.",
        "For rate = k[A]^m[B]^n, m and n are orders and k depends mainly on temperature and catalyst. The balanced equation alone usually cannot provide the exponents.",
        "if rate = k[A]^2, doubling [A] increases rate by 2^2 = four while other conditions remain fixed.",
        "rate = k[A]^m[B]^n",
        "Do not infer rate-law exponents from an overall balanced equation unless the step is explicitly elementary.",
        "Rate laws determine reactor size, shelf life, dosing intervals and safe process conditions.",
        listOf("if rate = k[A]^2, doubling [A] increases rate by 2^2 = four while other conditions remain fixed.","rate = k[A]^m[B]^n","For rate = k[A]^m[B]^n, m and n are orders and k depends mainly on temperature and catalyst.","The balanced equation alone usually cannot provide the exponents.","Do not infer rate-law exponents from an overall balanced equation unless the step is explicitly elementary."),
        listOf("Reaction order","Initial rates","Rate constant","Rate laws")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Kinetics and Electrochemistry|Collision theory and activation energy",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Collision theory explains reaction rate through collision frequency, sufficient energy and suitable molecular orientation.\n\nActivation energy is the minimum energy barrier between reactants and the transition-state region.",
        "Only collisions with energy at least equal to the activation energy and an effective orientation produce products. The Arrhenius factor exp(-Ea/RT) estimates the energetic fraction, while the pre-exponential factor includes collision frequency and orientation. Raising temperature increases the high-energy fraction strongly.\n\nA larger barrier gives a smaller rate constant at the same temperature. Catalysts speed reactions by providing a pathway with lower activation energy.",
        "two molecules may collide often but react slowly when Ea is high or their reactive sites do not meet. A catalyst supplies another pathway with lower Ea, increasing effective collisions without changing DeltaG° or K.\n\nExample: heating increases the fraction of molecules above Ea, so more collisions can cross the barrier and the reaction accelerates.",
        "k = A exp(-Ea/RT); effective collision = sufficient energy + correct orientation\n\nhigher Ea at fixed T -> smaller k",
        "Do not say every collision causes reaction or that a catalyst increases the energy of colliding molecules.\n\nActivation energy is not the reaction enthalpy; it controls rate rather than overall energy difference.",
        "Collision theory helps engineers choose temperature, catalysts and mixing conditions for safe and efficient reactors.\n\nBarrier control is crucial in combustion, enzymes, food preservation and industrial safety.",
        listOf("two molecules may collide often but react slowly when Ea is high or their reactive sites do not meet.","A catalyst supplies another pathway with lower Ea, increasing effective collisions without changing DeltaG° or K.","Example: heating increases the fraction of molecules above Ea, so more collisions can cross the barrier and the reaction accelerates.","k = A exp(-Ea/RT); effective collision = sufficient energy + correct orientation\n\nhigher Ea at fixed T -> smaller k","Only collisions with energy at least equal to the activation energy and an effective orientation produce products.","The Arrhenius factor exp(-Ea/RT) estimates the energetic fraction, while the pre-exponential factor includes collision frequency and orientation."),
        listOf("Activation energy","Arrhenius equation","Catalysis","Transition state","Collision theory")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Kinetics and Electrochemistry|Catalysts and reaction mechanisms",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Kinetic catalysis increases reaction rate through an alternative lower-energy mechanism.\n\nReaction mechanisms describe elementary bond-making and bond-breaking steps consistent with kinetics, stereochemistry and isotope evidence.",
        "A catalyst participates in elementary steps but is regenerated overall. It accelerates forward and reverse processes without changing deltaG, K or equilibrium composition.\n\nDescribe elementary bond-making and bond-breaking steps consistent with kinetics, stereochemistry and isotope evidence.",
        "MnO2 accelerates H2O2 decomposition by lowering the pathway barrier and remains chemically regenerated overall.\n\nAn SN2 substitution gives second-order kinetics and inversion at a chiral centre through backside attack.",
        "catalyst -> alternative pathway with lower activation energy\n\nrateSN2 = k[nucleophile][substrate]",
        "A catalyst does not increase equilibrium yield or make a thermodynamically impossible process favorable.\n\nAn overall balanced equation does not prove that the reaction occurs in one elementary collision.",
        "Catalysis enables lower-temperature manufacturing, emission control and enzyme-driven life chemistry.\n\nMechanistic comparisons explain why changing substrate, nucleophile or solvent alters organic product distribution.",
        listOf("MnO2 accelerates H2O2 decomposition by lowering the pathway barrier and remains chemically regenerated overall.","An SN2 substitution gives second-order kinetics and inversion at a chiral centre through backside attack.","catalyst -> alternative pathway with lower activation energy\n\nrateSN2 = k[nucleophile][substrate]","A catalyst participates in elementary steps but is regenerated overall.","It accelerates forward and reverse processes without changing deltaG, K or equilibrium composition.","Describe elementary bond-making and bond-breaking steps consistent with kinetics, stereochemistry and isotope evidence."),
        listOf("Mechanisms","Activation energy","Reaction rate","Catalysis","Reaction mechanisms")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Kinetics and Electrochemistry|Galvanic and electrolytic cells",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "A galvanic cell converts a spontaneous redox reaction into electrical work.\n\nElectrolysis uses electrical energy to drive a nonspontaneous redox reaction.",
        "Oxidation occurs at the anode, reduction at the cathode, electrons travel through the wire and ions through the electrolyte or salt bridge maintain charge balance.\n\nThe power supply pushes electrons, while ions migrate to electrodes. Deposited or released amount is proportional to charge passed.",
        "in a Daniell cell, Zn oxidizes at the anode and Cu2+ reduces at the cathode; electrons flow from Zn to Cu.\n\nExample: passing 193000 C through Cu2+ solution supplies 2 mol electrons and ideally deposits 1 mol copper.",
        "Ecell = Ecathode - Eanode\n\nQ = It; moles electrons = Q/F",
        "Oxidation is always at the anode and reduction at the cathode; do not rely only on electrode signs.\n\nElectrode signs differ from a galvanic cell, but oxidation remains at the anode and reduction at the cathode.",
        "Galvanic-cell principles underpin batteries, sensors and corrosion measurements.\n\nElectrolysis is used for plating, metal refining, chemical manufacture and hydrogen production.",
        listOf("in a Daniell cell, Zn oxidizes at the anode and Cu2+ reduces at the cathode","electrons flow from Zn to Cu.","Example: passing 193000 C through Cu2+ solution supplies 2 mol electrons and ideally deposits 1 mol copper.","Ecell = Ecathode - Eanode\n\nQ = It; moles electrons = Q/F","Oxidation occurs at the anode, reduction at the cathode, electrons travel through the wire and ions through the electrolyte or salt bridge maintain charge balance.","The power supply pushes electrons, while ions migrate to electrodes."),
        listOf("Cell notation","EMF","Salt bridge","Faraday laws","Electrodes","Nonspontaneous reactions","Galvanic cells","Electrolysis")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Kinetics and Electrochemistry|Batteries, corrosion and electrolysis",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Batteries store chemical free energy in coupled electrode reactions and deliver it as electrical work.\n\nCorrosion is unwanted electrochemical oxidation of a material by its environment.\n\nElectrolysis uses electrical energy to drive a nonspontaneous redox reaction.",
        "Lithium-ion capacity is estimated from reversible electron transfer per mass of active material.\n\nExample: zinc coating protects steel both as a barrier and, if scratched, by oxidizing sacrificially before iron.\n\nExample: passing 193000 C through Cu2+ solution supplies 2 mol electrons and ideally deposits 1 mol copper.",
        "Lithium-ion capacity is estimated from reversible electron transfer per mass of active material.\n\nExample: zinc coating protects steel both as a barrier and, if scratched, by oxidizing sacrificially before iron.\n\nExample: passing 193000 C through Cu2+ solution supplies 2 mol electrons and ideally deposits 1 mol copper.",
        "specific capacity = charge / active-material mass\n\nanode: Fe -> Fe2+ + 2e-\n\nQ = It; moles electrons = Q/F",
        "Electrons flow through the external circuit; ions carry charge through the electrolyte rather than the same electrons crossing both pathways.\n\nRusting requires an electrochemical pathway; oxygen alone without suitable moisture is insufficient for ordinary rust formation.\n\nElectrode signs differ from a galvanic cell, but oxidation remains at the anode and reduction at the cathode.",
        "Separated oxidation and reduction half-reactions store and deliver electrical energy while ion transport maintains charge balance.\n\nCorrosion science protects bridges, pipelines, vehicles, ships and electronic equipment.\n\nElectrolysis is used for plating, metal refining, chemical manufacture and hydrogen production.",
        listOf("Lithium-ion capacity is estimated from reversible electron transfer per mass of active material.","Example: zinc coating protects steel both as a barrier and, if scratched, by oxidizing sacrificially before iron.","Example: passing 193000 C through Cu2+ solution supplies 2 mol electrons and ideally deposits 1 mol copper.","specific capacity = charge / active-material mass\n\nanode: Fe -> Fe2+ + 2e-\n\nQ = It; moles electrons = Q/F","Electrons flow through the external circuit; ions carry charge through the electrolyte rather than the same electrons crossing both pathways.\n\nRusting requires an electrochemical pathway; oxygen alone without suitable moisture is insufficient for ordinary rust formation.\n\nElectrode signs differ from a galvanic cell, but oxidation remains at the anode and reduction at the cathode."),
        listOf("Materials Chemistry","Sacrificial protection","Galvanization","Electrochemical cells","Faraday laws","Electrodes","Nonspontaneous reactions","Batteries","Corrosion","Electrolysis")),
